[1m[38;5;84m  ◆ FINDING WHAT OWNS A PORT[0m[38;5;214m[0m

  Short answer: [1m[38;5;84mss[0m
  on Linux, [38;5;214mlsof[0m on
  macOS. Both show the
  [3m[38;5;41mlistening[0m socket
  and the PID that
  holds it.
[1m[38;5;84m  ◆ THE OPTIONS[0m[38;5;214m───────[0m

[38;5;84m ▸ [0m[38;5;214mss -ltnp[0m lists TCP
   listeners with
   process names; needs
   root to see other
   users' processes.
[38;5;84m ▸ [0m[38;5;214mlsof -nP -iTCP:8080 -sTCP:LISTEN[0m
   works on both
   platforms and is the
   most portable choice
   you have.
[38;5;84m ▸ [0m[38;5;214mnetstat -tulpn[0m is
   the legacy tool,
   deprecated in favour
   of [1m[38;5;84miproute2[0m on
   every modern distro.
[1m[38;5;84m  ◆ STEPS[0m[38;5;214m─────────────[0m

[38;5;84m1.[0m Run the command
   below.
[38;5;84m2.[0m Read the
   [1m[38;5;84mPID/Program name[0m
   column — that is
   your culprit, and
   [38;5;214mps -fp <pid>[0m tells
   you who started it.
[38;5;84m10.[0m Kill it politely
    with [38;5;214mkill <pid>[0m
    before reaching for
    [38;5;214m-9[0m.
